1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:06,440
It's one of history's deadliest jobs, fighting the flames to save lives and property.

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:10,120
It's even tougher when fire breaks out under extreme conditions.

3
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:14,460
Fighting fires here requires unconventional tools and techniques.

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:17,560
Throwing dynamite into a roaring oil well.

5
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:19,920
Parachuting into a blazing wilderness.

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:22,980
Smothering a flaming airplane with foam.

7
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:27,740
Up next on Modern Marvels, firefighting in extreme conditions.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
An oil well fire is like no other spectacle on Earth.

9
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:54,400
A blowtorch fueled by a rocket of oil and gas blasting out of control.

10
00:00:54,401 --> 00:01:06,140
A blowing well that has caught fire is a perfect example of Mother Nature out of control.

11
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:13,380
To see this column of fire three or four hundred feet up in the air, it's a pretty awesome spectacle.

12
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:15,614
It is amazing.

13
00:01:15,615 --> 00:01:21,000
It's amazing that Mother Nature can put that much energy coming from the earth.

14
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:27,160
almost more impressive than the heat is the sound

15
00:01:27,161 --> 00:01:35,640
you feel as though you're being bombarded you're hit after a while you just get sort of numb and

16
00:01:35,641 --> 00:01:43,480
you can't get numb while you're working on a fire to work on a well you certainly can't be afraid of

17
00:01:43,481 --> 00:01:50,440
but you have to respect it all the time because it will kill you

18
00:01:50,441 --> 00:01:57,440
it requires a special breed to tame a burning wild well

19
00:01:57,441 --> 00:02:02,960
it also takes firefighting's most unconventional tool dynamite

20
00:02:02,961 --> 00:02:16,961
it's a risky technique first developed in an era when the world's oil fields were the devil's

21
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:18,220
playground

22
00:02:18,221 --> 00:02:32,221
oil well fires are as old as the oil business itself when an oil man cried gusher it wasn't

23
00:02:34,580 --> 00:02:43,620
necessarily good news it often meant his drilling rig was out of control in those days there wasn't

24
00:02:43,621 --> 00:02:46,264
whole lot to do to control a well.

25
00:02:46,265 --> 00:02:50,620
Those wells were allowed to blow and if they caught fire

26
00:02:50,621 --> 00:02:55,760
they sometimes burned for years.

27
00:02:55,761 --> 00:03:01,000
Blowouts occur when drillers underestimate the pressure of oil and gas underground known

28
00:03:01,001 --> 00:03:02,374
as geopressure.

29
00:03:02,375 --> 00:03:08,880
Engineers try to compensate for geopressure by injecting heavy mud into

30
00:03:08,881 --> 00:03:10,952
the hole as they drill.

31
00:03:10,953 --> 00:03:17,260
But sometimes the mud isn't heavy enough to hold back the oil and gas.

32
00:03:17,740 --> 00:03:20,261
The result is a blowout.

33
00:03:20,262 --> 00:03:27,300
Blowouts can twist a drilling derrick into a maze of jagged debris.

34
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:34,760
A spark or the heat from the motor or light bulb can set the gusher ablaze, bringing catastrophe.

35
00:03:34,761 --> 00:03:43,940
These wells were within 100 feet of each other, and so if one well caught fire, well, all the wells downwind caught fire.

36
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:52,520
Early on, crews injected steam into the flames, hoping the vapor would cool the blaze and cause it to stomp.

37
00:03:54,860 --> 00:04:00,940
But in 1913, a family from Southern California proposed a radical new approach.

38
00:04:00,941 --> 00:04:06,000
Carl Kinley was handy with nitroglycerin

39
00:04:06,001 --> 00:04:08,960
As a professional shooter

40
00:04:08,961 --> 00:04:11,760
Kinley triggered explosions underground

41
00:04:11,761 --> 00:04:15,240
To increase the flow of oil on stubborn wells

42
00:04:15,241 --> 00:04:23,640
Kinley had a theory about fire

43
00:04:23,641 --> 00:04:27,380
I concede the idea of shooting out the flame

44
00:04:27,381 --> 00:04:32,660
The idea is just like that of blowing out a candle or an oil lamp.

45
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:36,500
The force of the breath blows the blaze from the fuel.

46
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:41,280
I figured if I could get a strong enough breath, I could do it.

47
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:43,000
Carl Kinley.

48
00:04:45,620 --> 00:04:52,960
On April 29, 1913, Kinley and an assistant tossed a nitroglycerin bomb by hand

49
00:04:52,961 --> 00:04:56,420
into the wreckage of a burning well in Taft, California.

50
00:04:57,380 --> 00:05:05,660
They placed the shot and it blew the derrick away and it also blew out the fire.

51
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:11,380
Kinley was unhurt and his shot was heard round the world.

52
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:20,820
A shot witnessed by Kinley's son Myron who would perfect this revolutionary technique as he took over the family business.

53
00:05:20,821 --> 00:05:32,040
in the 1920s and 30s Myron Kinley surpassed his father to become the world's premier

54
00:05:32,041 --> 00:05:40,380
wild well specialist Myron looked on the fire as an enemy and he found it very satisfying

55
00:05:40,381 --> 00:05:49,420
to know that he could put them out Kinley moved to Texas and recruited a team of daredevil assistants

56
00:05:49,421 --> 00:05:55,600
men who would eventually become the superstars of the oil well firefighting industry.

57
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,020
Well, you know, we all have a lot of respect for Mr. Kinley.

58
00:06:01,021 --> 00:06:06,060
We called him the grandfather of the oil well firefighting business, which I suppose he was.

59
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:13,060
Kinley pioneered the three-step process of bringing a wild well under control.

60
00:06:13,061 --> 00:06:21,660
First, crews must remove the twisted debris that causes the flames to shoot in several directions.

61
00:06:21,661 --> 00:06:28,460
To work on the wellhead itself is hard to do underneath a drilling rig.

62
00:06:28,461 --> 00:06:32,460
It's very dangerous because something on top may fall.

63
00:06:32,461 --> 00:06:36,560
So the first thing you got to do is prepare to move the drilling rig off there.

64
00:06:36,561 --> 00:06:43,560
With bulldozers and booms, crews methodically drag away the drill pipe and other debris.

65
00:06:43,561 --> 00:06:54,560
When the debris is gone, it's time for the second step, preparing the explosives.

66
00:06:54,561 --> 00:07:01,560
We'll adjust the explosive to the fire, the height of the fire, the diameter of the fire.

67
00:07:01,561 --> 00:07:05,700
A lot of the shots are 150 pounds, some are 250.

68
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:11,860
In a 55-gallon drum, you can put 497 sticks of dynamite.

69
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:19,320
The explosive charge wrapped in heat-resistant asbestos is backed into place.

70
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:36,280
The concussion from the shock knocks the oxygen away,

71
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:38,360
and fire don't burn without oxygen.

72
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:42,180
By the time the oxygen gets back, the fire is out,

73
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:45,300
and the water is keeping it cool, so it shouldn't start back fire.

74
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:54,080
With the fire out, crews begin the third part of the process,

75
00:07:54,660 --> 00:07:55,880
capping the blown-out well.

76
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:00,540
Working with brass tools to avoid sparks,

77
00:08:00,541 --> 00:08:03,040
the men replaced the damaged wellhead.

78
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:10,220
For this part of the job, firefighters hope for a windy day.

79
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:15,220
If there's no wind blowing and the gas is laying close to the ground,

80
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:18,700
it's very frightening because the whole thing can just ignite.

81
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:26,360
It can take weeks to bring a burning blowout under control.

82
00:08:26,361 --> 00:08:33,600
Although the process is well established, no two fires are ever alike.

83
00:08:33,601 --> 00:08:38,880
And occasionally, firefighters encounter a problem they've never experienced before.

84
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:46,420
That was the case in 1991 in Kuwait.

85
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:55,580
The Persian Gulf War created the biggest oil well firefighting challenge in history.

86
00:08:56,360 --> 00:09:07,360
Iraqi troops set more than 700 wells ablaze before United Nations forces drove them from Kuwait during Operation Desert Storm.

87
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:16,360
Every wild well specialist in the world was called upon to battle the fires.

88
00:09:16,361 --> 00:09:22,760
fires we'd never seen anything like that nobody had and it was an awesome looking sight that you

89
00:09:22,761 --> 00:09:28,120
could be standing out there midday and the smoke was so bad you couldn't see your shoes

90
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:39,640
with so many wells on fire kuwait became a proving ground for a wide range of new technologies

91
00:09:39,641 --> 00:09:44,640
Hungarian crews used a vehicle with twin jet engines.

92
00:09:44,641 --> 00:09:52,640
They literally blew fires out with thousands of pounds of thrust.

93
00:09:52,641 --> 00:10:06,640
Kuwaitis used high volume water pumps, which delivered more than 12,000 gallons per minute.

94
00:10:06,641 --> 00:10:20,641
minute. The force of the water was enough to also blow fires out.

95
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:30,880
US crews used long metal smokestacks that allowed them to work on damaged well heads

96
00:10:30,881 --> 00:10:36,120
without having to put the fire out. If you go over the fire with your capping

97
00:10:36,121 --> 00:10:43,361
stack, then we're raising that flame above us approximately 30 to 40 feet.

98
00:10:43,362 --> 00:10:44,340
And it's a

99
00:10:44,341 --> 00:10:46,880
lot cooler for us to work under it and a lot safer.

100
00:10:46,881 --> 00:10:48,040
There it goes.

101
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:55,580
By allowing the fire to burn until a new wellhead was in place, crews can minimize damage to

102
00:10:55,581 --> 00:10:56,808
the environment.

103
00:10:56,809 --> 00:11:02,180
The fire consumes much of the oil, avoiding a toxic pool of pollution.

104
00:11:06,120 --> 00:11:11,180
It took seven months to extinguish the Kuwaiti oil fires.

105
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:15,640
Never before had so many wells been on fire at once.

106
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:21,640
Remarkably, despite the danger, no firefighters were killed.

107
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:32,800
Up next, battling America's forest fires,

108
00:11:32,801 --> 00:11:37,700
from the days of picks and shovels to the modern aerial assault.

109
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:47,420
Nearly 80% of Kuwait's oil wells were set ablaze.

110
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:52,940
To extinguish the fires, firefighters had to build 280 kilometers of new roads

111
00:11:52,941 --> 00:11:57,500
to transport their 145,000 tons of firefighting equipment.

112
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:00,280
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

113
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:14,360
Fire has always been part of the American landscape, serving an important ecological

114
00:12:14,361 --> 00:12:17,360
role.

115
00:12:17,361 --> 00:12:22,860
Occasional fires sparked by lightning creep slowly along the forest floor, cleansing

116
00:12:22,861 --> 00:12:28,780
the woods of debris.

117
00:12:28,781 --> 00:12:36,920
This natural process was disrupted in the 1800s by reckless pioneers.

118
00:12:36,921 --> 00:12:41,400
By the late 1800s fire was a plague on the land.

119
00:12:41,401 --> 00:12:47,100
Major forest fires were touched off by the sparks from steam locomotives and careless

120
00:12:47,101 --> 00:12:54,300
burning by farmers when clearing land for crops.

121
00:12:54,301 --> 00:12:59,420
In 1871, a forest fire wiped out the farming town of Peshtigo, Wisconsin,

122
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:02,380
claiming more than a thousand lives.

123
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:07,460
These fires did a great deal of damage and provided part of the background

124
00:13:07,461 --> 00:13:12,220
for thinking that the country could not continue to burn itself up in this way.

125
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:17,320
It was no longer the friendly flame or an extension of the hearth,

126
00:13:17,321 --> 00:13:19,360
and it had become a kind of holocaust.

127
00:13:19,361 --> 00:13:27,240
President Theodore Roosevelt took action on March 3rd, 1905

128
00:13:27,241 --> 00:13:30,520
by federalizing large tracts of land

129
00:13:30,521 --> 00:13:32,880
and creating the U.S. Forest Service

130
00:13:32,881 --> 00:13:38,700
The agency's first chief, Gifford Pinchot

131
00:13:38,701 --> 00:13:42,700
was told to conquer forest fires at any cost

132
00:13:42,701 --> 00:13:47,120
Pinchot called fire the Dragon Devastation

133
00:13:47,121 --> 00:13:54,640
And so he could imagine a kind of crusade or a forester, if you will, is knight errant out to slay this dragon,

134
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:59,400
which was destroying the countryside and breathing fire, as mythical dragons would.

135
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:03,080
Early efforts were not successful.

136
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:07,680
Armed only with hand tools, firefighters died by the dozen.

137
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:16,020
Technology slowly began to even the odds.

138
00:14:17,120 --> 00:14:24,720
In the 1920s, the Forest Service began aerial patrols with radio relays to the ground.

139
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:32,980
Foresters knew if they could spot a fire quickly, they'd have a better chance at putting it out.

140
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:39,680
Still, spotting a fire and getting to it were separate problems.

141
00:14:42,340 --> 00:14:45,840
You want to get to these fires as early as possible.

142
00:14:45,841 --> 00:14:48,880
The longer they linger on the landscape, the bigger they become.

143
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:53,320
But in areas without extensive roads and trails,

144
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:56,540
you need some other way to get people in,

145
00:14:56,600 --> 00:14:59,460
and so you begin dropping them from planes.

146
00:15:02,780 --> 00:15:07,540
In 1939, the Forest Service began an air war on fire.

147
00:15:08,540 --> 00:15:10,300
The smokejumpers were born.

148
00:15:10,301 --> 00:15:18,740
smokejumper boot camps prepared the recruits for the perils of skydiving

149
00:15:18,741 --> 00:15:26,760
smokejumpers were among the first people to ever use parachutes

150
00:15:33,760 --> 00:15:39,040
each man wore a padded canvas jumpsuit to protect him if he landed in a tree

151
00:15:39,041 --> 00:15:51,800
The protective headgear looked like a cross between a football helmet and a catcher's mask

152
00:15:57,440 --> 00:16:02,080
Smoke jumpers could reach a small remote fire in a matter of hours

153
00:16:02,081 --> 00:16:11,020
on the scene their strategy was simple

154
00:16:11,021 --> 00:16:16,020
scrape a circle around the fire to remove the fuel it needs to advance

155
00:16:16,021 --> 00:16:20,100
this technique is still in use today

156
00:16:20,101 --> 00:16:24,580
what we would do is that we'd start at the foot of the fire

157
00:16:24,581 --> 00:16:28,300
and we'd cut fuel breaks or what we refer to as fire line

158
00:16:28,301 --> 00:16:32,000
from the back of the fire along the flanks and then eventually try

159
00:16:32,001 --> 00:16:35,660
to pinch off that very active front or head of the fire.

160
00:16:37,460 --> 00:16:40,480
With fire lines established around a blaze,

161
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:43,280
smoke jumpers fight fire with fire.

162
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:48,760
By setting small controlled backfires near the fire line,

163
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:50,540
they widen the dead zone.

164
00:16:53,720 --> 00:16:57,080
When the forest fire arrives, there's nothing left to burn.

165
00:16:57,860 --> 00:16:59,200
The fire goes out.

166
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:09,000
Today's firefighters have the benefit of decades of technological advances.

167
00:17:09,001 --> 00:17:19,000
To predict fire behavior, to battle the blaze more efficiently and work with a greater margin of safety.

168
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Bulldozers are now used to rapidly cut fire lines.

169
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:46,000
Helicopters can pump water from nearby lakes and drop it directly on the flames.

170
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:56,000
Cargo planes can drop even heavier loads.

171
00:17:56,001 --> 00:18:01,000
The super scooper can make a touch and go landing on the lake or in the ocean and carry

172
00:18:01,001 --> 00:18:09,000
1600 gallons of water to the flames.

173
00:18:09,001 --> 00:18:14,000
On some runs the pilots mix water with a special fire retarding chemical.

174
00:18:14,001 --> 00:18:20,000
The reddish mixture also contains fertilizer and seed to promote rapid plant growth and

175
00:18:20,001 --> 00:18:25,000
fire damaged terrain.

176
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:33,000
Today's smoke jumpers have better equipment too.

177
00:18:33,001 --> 00:18:40,000
Jumpsuits are made of Kevlar, the fabric used in bulletproof vests.

178
00:18:40,001 --> 00:18:45,000
Every jumper carries an emergency heat-resistant shelter.

179
00:18:45,001 --> 00:18:49,000
It's like a pup tent.

180
00:18:49,001 --> 00:18:52,000
You spread it.

181
00:18:52,001 --> 00:19:00,220
You crawl inside of it and you anchor it down with your hands in leather gloves and of course you'd be in your firefighting clothing.

182
00:19:01,180 --> 00:19:06,080
Spin it around and get inside of it.

183
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:07,960
About the size of a person.

184
00:19:11,980 --> 00:19:15,860
The survival shelter is made of fiberglass and aluminum.

185
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:21,100
Firefighters inside them can survive temperatures of 500 degrees.

186
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:27,000
Some firefighters call the shelter their shake and bake shell.

187
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:51,000
Despite the impressive array of equipment to fight forest fires, sometimes fires still win.

188
00:19:52,000 --> 00:20:03,260
In 1988, nearly one and a half million acres of Yellowstone National Park went up in smoke.

189
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:09,560
More than 9,000 firefighters battled the blaze.

190
00:20:12,140 --> 00:20:15,780
Helicopters dropped more than 10 million gallons of water.

191
00:20:16,980 --> 00:20:18,640
But nothing worked.

192
00:20:18,641 --> 00:20:21,260
We had an enormous technological armada,

193
00:20:21,680 --> 00:20:25,080
and the fires burned as they were going to burn regardless.

194
00:20:28,740 --> 00:20:31,420
Generations of successful spot firefighting

195
00:20:31,421 --> 00:20:34,540
had allowed too much debris to build up in Yellowstone Park.

196
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:38,960
When fire broke out in a drought-stricken year,

197
00:20:39,620 --> 00:20:40,740
it was unstoppable.

198
00:20:44,620 --> 00:20:46,780
As long as we have public wildlands,

199
00:20:46,860 --> 00:20:48,400
we are going to have wildland fire.

200
00:20:48,640 --> 00:20:50,380
from one source or another.

201
00:20:50,381 --> 00:20:53,600
And the only issue is what kind of fire we have.

202
00:20:53,601 --> 00:20:55,520
If you put out all these little fires

203
00:20:55,521 --> 00:20:56,820
that are easy to take care of

204
00:20:56,821 --> 00:20:59,260
and burning through the surface,

205
00:20:59,261 --> 00:21:02,440
you create conditions in many environments

206
00:21:02,441 --> 00:21:05,020
where you can create much larger fires.

207
00:21:07,780 --> 00:21:11,440
In many places, officials now let fires burn

208
00:21:11,441 --> 00:21:15,580
or set small fires themselves to mimic nature's fire cycle.

209
00:21:18,640 --> 00:21:32,640
While this strategy may work in a remote wilderness, authorities face a dilemma when forest fires threaten homes and businesses.

210
00:21:32,641 --> 00:21:41,640
As suburbs move farther and farther into wild country, the threat of fire disaster grows larger.

211
00:21:41,641 --> 00:21:52,460
This problem surfaced in the 1960s when a brush fire raced through the fashionable Brentwood and Bel Air sections of Los Angeles

212
00:21:52,461 --> 00:22:00,520
Nearly 500 homes were destroyed

213
00:22:00,521 --> 00:22:13,960
forest and brush fires have continued to play Los Angeles and other sprawling metropolitan

214
00:22:13,961 --> 00:22:23,380
communities it's a problem that won't be easy to solve the solution is not really a technological

215
00:22:23,381 --> 00:22:29,740
one the solution is one of zoning of planning of building structures out of non-combustible

216
00:22:29,741 --> 00:22:30,441
materials.

217
00:22:30,442 --> 00:22:35,180
As long as you build houses or towns out of forest materials, they're going

218
00:22:35,181 --> 00:22:38,500
to burn just like forest fires.

219
00:22:38,501 --> 00:22:48,180
Up next, battlefield firefighting, including the story of the heroes who saved London from

220
00:22:48,181 --> 00:22:56,100
total destruction during the Nazi firebombing blitz.

221
00:22:56,101 --> 00:23:00,420
Squire Boone, brother of Daniel Boone, invented the first fire extinguisher.

222
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:03,500
He forced water through a gun barrel with a wooden rod

223
00:23:03,501 --> 00:23:08,080
and used it during Indian attacks to extinguish flaming arrows on the roof of his fort.

224
00:23:08,780 --> 00:23:10,600
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

225
00:23:14,680 --> 00:23:18,040
Fire has been one of history's most potent weapons.

226
00:23:20,820 --> 00:23:25,380
Perhaps nowhere was fire more deadly than the battlefields of World War II.

227
00:23:26,100 --> 00:23:33,100
flame throwing tanks could burn an enemy to death more than a hundred yards away

228
00:23:33,101 --> 00:23:39,100
no place witnessed more heroic firefighting during world war two than

229
00:23:39,101 --> 00:23:43,100
london during the nazi bombing blitz

230
00:23:43,101 --> 00:23:50,100
after the fall of france in 1940 the british knew trouble was coming

231
00:23:50,101 --> 00:23:55,300
In 1940, the British knew trouble was coming.

232
00:23:55,301 --> 00:24:00,840
The British recognized the need to prepare for possible invasion and possible bombing

233
00:24:00,841 --> 00:24:05,860
of their cities, and they did take a number of steps to be prepared, and those steps in

234
00:24:05,861 --> 00:24:09,920
the end did pay off.

235
00:24:09,921 --> 00:24:16,560
Authorities recruited 273,000 men and women to be firefighters, positioning them strategically

236
00:24:16,561 --> 00:24:24,980
throughout the city. London quadrupled its fleet of fireboats from five ships to 20.

237
00:24:31,280 --> 00:24:36,880
When the bombing of London began, the German Luftwaffe tried night after night to inflict

238
00:24:36,881 --> 00:24:37,787
maximum damage.

239
00:24:37,788 --> 00:24:41,960
Down below, the London sky watchers were anxiously probing the night.

240
00:24:41,961 --> 00:24:52,729
All-out air bombardment was by now starkly familiar to Londoners, yet each time it was so dreadfully new, each new shower of incendiary so terrifying.

241
00:24:52,730 --> 00:24:55,960
Here's how it went the night of May 10, 1941.

242
00:24:55,961 --> 00:25:05,720
A typical incendiary attack would start somewhere in the vicinity of 2,000 fires,

243
00:25:06,080 --> 00:25:08,620
and that occurred every single night.

244
00:25:08,760 --> 00:25:13,840
So the challenges that faced the firefighters of London and the surrounding communities was incredible.

245
00:25:15,920 --> 00:25:21,160
The London Fire Brigades faced an entire city ablaze.

246
00:25:21,760 --> 00:25:24,420
We were going back over London Bridge,

247
00:25:24,421 --> 00:25:28,600
and this was nighttime, you see, and we stopped for a moment

248
00:25:28,601 --> 00:25:31,900
and we looked to the left and we looked to the right

249
00:25:31,901 --> 00:25:36,180
and it was the most magnificent sight that you could see.

250
00:25:37,260 --> 00:25:39,820
If it hadn't been so tragic, it was wonderful.

251
00:25:41,400 --> 00:25:44,720
It looked as if all London had been burnt down.

252
00:25:49,720 --> 00:25:53,440
Not only are you trying to deal with buildings that are on fire

253
00:25:53,441 --> 00:25:54,780
and collapse at any moment.

254
00:25:55,100 --> 00:25:57,100
But bombs are dropping around you.

255
00:25:57,800 --> 00:25:58,960
Buildings are blowing up.

256
00:25:59,440 --> 00:26:01,600
Not only that, there are bombs that haven't exploded,

257
00:26:02,180 --> 00:26:04,860
that there's still live bombs that somebody needs to detonate.

258
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:12,440
One of the biggest challenges in fighting the fires was water.

259
00:26:14,700 --> 00:26:17,000
Bombs destroyed London's water pipes.

260
00:26:19,900 --> 00:26:22,520
The British, however, expected this to happen

261
00:26:22,521 --> 00:26:25,180
and built thousands of portable water tanks.

262
00:26:28,920 --> 00:26:33,380
Firefighters could construct an emergency water system on the spot.

263
00:26:37,940 --> 00:26:41,000
The London Blitz lasted eight months.

264
00:26:45,900 --> 00:26:48,580
14,000 people were killed.

265
00:26:48,581 --> 00:26:54,500
British Prime Minister Winston Churchill credited firefighters

266
00:26:54,501 --> 00:26:57,980
with saving large portions of London from ruin

267
00:26:57,981 --> 00:27:01,600
calling them heroes with grimy faces

268
00:27:01,601 --> 00:27:06,820
If the British Empire and its Commonwealth

269
00:27:06,821 --> 00:27:09,600
last for a thousand years

270
00:27:09,601 --> 00:27:12,040
men will still say

271
00:27:12,041 --> 00:27:16,120
this was their fighting tower

272
00:27:16,121 --> 00:27:23,320
Up next, terror at the airports.

273
00:27:24,320 --> 00:27:30,640
What firefighters do to save lives when airplanes crash and burn.

274
00:27:35,040 --> 00:27:40,160
The oxygen vacuum created by large fires can produce hurricane force winds.

275
00:27:40,161 --> 00:27:46,380
Fire bombings in World War II reportedly created winds strong enough to collapse buildings and uproot trees.

276
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:48,760
Modern marvels will return in a moment.

277
00:27:53,060 --> 00:27:58,560
There are 160,000 takeoffs and landings every day in the United States.

278
00:28:04,520 --> 00:28:07,220
Nearly every one is perfect.

279
00:28:07,940 --> 00:28:13,940
However, when things go wrong at the airport, there's an added dimension of terror.

280
00:28:27,860 --> 00:28:32,100
Aircraft fires date back to the dawn of commercial aviation.

281
00:28:33,080 --> 00:28:35,080
May 6th, 1937.

282
00:28:35,081 --> 00:28:36,880
the Hindenburg.

283
00:28:36,881 --> 00:28:39,760
The ship is riding modestly shorter.

284
00:28:40,200 --> 00:28:41,820
It's practically standing still now.

285
00:28:41,880 --> 00:28:44,400
They've dropped rope out of the nose of the ship.

286
00:28:44,960 --> 00:28:46,120
Get it started. Get it started.

287
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:48,040
It's burning, bursting into flames,

288
00:28:48,160 --> 00:28:49,760
and it's falling on the mooring sands,

289
00:28:49,840 --> 00:28:51,680
and all the folks with me that this is terrible.

290
00:28:51,800 --> 00:28:53,940
This is one of the worst catastrophes in the world.

291
00:28:54,300 --> 00:28:56,240
It's a terrific crash later still,

292
00:28:56,320 --> 00:28:58,000
but it's broken and it's slaves now.

293
00:28:58,320 --> 00:28:59,460
All the humanity.

294
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:05,160
In 32 seconds,

295
00:29:05,161 --> 00:29:07,080
35 people were dead.

296
00:29:15,980 --> 00:29:19,640
Surprisingly though, 63 passengers and crew members

297
00:29:19,641 --> 00:29:22,040
survived the Hindenburg disaster.

298
00:29:26,120 --> 00:29:29,260
Even today, as bad as a crash looks,

299
00:29:29,620 --> 00:29:33,600
more than 80% of the passengers involved in aircraft fires

300
00:29:33,601 --> 00:29:34,301
survived.

301
00:29:35,160 --> 00:29:38,600
People are not necessarily doomed in an airplane crash.

302
00:29:38,601 --> 00:29:43,460
If the plane drops out of the sky, yes, there's probably a lot of problems there with survivability.

303
00:29:43,461 --> 00:29:51,000
If it has an accident on landing or has an in-flight emergency and then lands with an in-flight emergency, it's very survivable.

304
00:29:51,001 --> 00:29:55,320
The key to survival is rapid response.

305
00:29:55,321 --> 00:30:00,460
Fire can burn through the fuselage between one minute and a minute and a half.

306
00:30:00,461 --> 00:30:05,400
So if the fire is directly impinging on the fuselage on impact,

307
00:30:05,760 --> 00:30:08,080
we've got a very short time to get there.

308
00:30:10,040 --> 00:30:15,080
Airplane fires require different equipment and tactics than house fires,

309
00:30:15,800 --> 00:30:19,960
primarily because airliners carry thousands of gallons of fuel.

310
00:30:21,840 --> 00:30:26,020
Firefighters use a mix of water and foam to snuff out the fire.

311
00:30:26,021 --> 00:30:32,140
foam prevents oxygen from reaching the flames

312
00:30:32,141 --> 00:30:42,460
airport fire trucks are fully self-sufficient ready to pump the moment they arrive

313
00:30:42,461 --> 00:30:50,820
the rescue vehicles that we have today are racehorses it can do zero to 50 miles an hour

314
00:30:50,821 --> 00:30:52,060
in about 21 seconds.

315
00:30:52,540 --> 00:30:55,820
It carries over 1,000 gallons of extinguishing agent

316
00:30:55,821 --> 00:30:58,100
and 500 pounds of dry chemical.

317
00:30:58,760 --> 00:31:01,800
And it can go anywhere on or off the airfield property.

318
00:31:01,900 --> 00:31:05,440
It doesn't have to be on improved cement or blacktop.

319
00:31:05,640 --> 00:31:06,340
It can go anywhere.

320
00:31:08,480 --> 00:31:12,760
The latest innovation is a 55-foot telescoping boom.

321
00:31:15,060 --> 00:31:19,320
One truck can quickly cover an entire aircraft.

322
00:31:20,820 --> 00:31:29,220
To take the fire attack inside an airplane, the boom can poke through the fuselage.

323
00:31:31,920 --> 00:31:35,780
Should there ever be a need to provide interior fire suppression,

324
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:40,100
we can inject water at the rate of 375 gallons a minute

325
00:31:40,101 --> 00:31:45,180
through this pipe into the interior of the cabin very early into the event.

326
00:31:45,181 --> 00:31:58,180
Engineers at the Federal Aviation Administration, the FAA, are working on other ways to make airplane fires less dangerous.

327
00:32:00,180 --> 00:32:05,180
This is the FAA Research Center in Atlantic City, New Jersey.

328
00:32:06,180 --> 00:32:10,180
Here, scientists set real airplanes ablaze.

329
00:32:10,181 --> 00:32:21,960
The most reliable way of studying fire and developing improvements is to actually set the real thing on fire in a very realistic fashion.

330
00:32:23,160 --> 00:32:30,040
Full-scale tests document the way flammable gases build up inside an airplane cabin.

331
00:32:35,060 --> 00:32:37,960
Smoke begins to build from the ceiling downward.

332
00:32:40,180 --> 00:32:47,180
Escape is possible for three minutes until a phenomenon occurs called flashover.

333
00:32:47,181 --> 00:32:58,180
The heat from the fuel fire as well as the heat radiated from the smoke layer raise the temperature of these materials to the point where they spontaneously ignite.

334
00:32:58,181 --> 00:33:05,180
The combustion will occur so rapidly that it will actually starve the oxygen in the air,

335
00:33:05,181 --> 00:33:11,180
and the conditions throughout the cabin will become virtually non-survivable within a matter of seconds.

336
00:33:14,180 --> 00:33:19,180
These tests have led to regulations requiring flame-resistant airplane seats,

337
00:33:19,181 --> 00:33:23,180
which reduce the buildup of combustible gas inside a burning plane.

338
00:33:28,180 --> 00:33:35,180
FAA research has also developed equipment to stop fires inside airplane baggage compartments.

339
00:33:37,180 --> 00:33:43,180
Most airlines have systems to flood the compartment with Halon gas when smoke alarms go off.

340
00:33:45,180 --> 00:33:52,180
Halon chemically prevents oxygen from reacting with flammable materials, eliminating the fire.

341
00:33:58,180 --> 00:34:12,180
not all FAA experiments have gone according to plan

342
00:34:12,800 --> 00:34:21,560
in the 1970s and 80s scientists tried to develop a safer formula for jet fuel

343
00:34:21,561 --> 00:34:31,740
when an airplane crashes jet fuel forms a highly flammable mist that erupts into a deadly fireball

344
00:34:31,741 --> 00:34:42,420
this fireball mist will track and follow and attach itself to the wing or the tail of the

345
00:34:42,421 --> 00:34:49,280
airplane and that becomes an inferno entirely around the outside of the fuselage so people

346
00:34:49,281 --> 00:34:53,360
that are in the cabin have survived the impact but yet can't get out

347
00:35:00,720 --> 00:35:06,320
in the 1970s chemists developed an additive that stops fuel from forming a mist

348
00:35:08,320 --> 00:35:13,120
on december 1st 1984 researchers prepared for the ultimate test

349
00:35:13,121 --> 00:35:17,960
the crash of a remote-controlled Boeing 720.

350
00:35:18,960 --> 00:35:24,220
We had spent, oh, six or seven years preparing for this one experiment

351
00:35:24,221 --> 00:35:30,420
that would demonstrate the properties that we had seen in other lower-scale tests.

352
00:35:32,100 --> 00:35:36,620
As the jet came in for a controlled wheels-up landing on its belly,

353
00:35:37,540 --> 00:35:38,560
something went wrong.

354
00:35:43,120 --> 00:35:50,420
The engine exploded, all the fuel dumped right on this exploding inferno of an engine,

355
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:53,540
and there was an immediate fireball.

356
00:35:55,620 --> 00:35:58,140
The crash hadn't gone as planned.

357
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:03,040
However, the fire would have been worse if the plane had used regular jet fuel

358
00:36:03,041 --> 00:36:05,160
instead of the experimental formulation.

359
00:36:05,161 --> 00:36:13,540
cameras inside showed most passengers would have survived

360
00:36:13,541 --> 00:36:21,280
still the test and ultimately the fuel additive program were regarded as failures

361
00:36:21,281 --> 00:36:34,000
despite the fuel program setback today's airliners are the most fire worthy planes ever built

362
00:36:35,160 --> 00:36:44,420
Accidents are inevitable, but more and more passengers are surviving airline disasters

363
00:36:44,421 --> 00:36:49,540
because of the efforts of aircraft engineers and airport fire departments.

364
00:36:53,260 --> 00:36:59,540
Chemical additives, such as water wetter, can increase water's natural penetrability by 300%,

365
00:36:59,541 --> 00:37:04,420
allowing firefighters to put out smoldering fires with one-third the normal amount of water.

366
00:37:04,421 --> 00:37:06,920
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

367
00:37:10,760 --> 00:37:12,420
January 1967.

368
00:37:13,740 --> 00:37:16,720
Apollo 1, America's first moonshot.

369
00:37:17,780 --> 00:37:24,160
Virgil Grissom, Edward White, and Roger Chaffee die on launch pad 34.

370
00:37:26,260 --> 00:37:31,220
An electrical fire consumes their capsule during a training exercise.

371
00:37:34,420 --> 00:37:40,180
January 1986, the space shuttle Challenger.

372
00:37:41,240 --> 00:37:46,620
A fuel fire destroys the ship 71 seconds after launch.

373
00:37:53,940 --> 00:37:56,700
Seven astronauts are killed.

374
00:37:56,701 --> 00:38:06,120
NASA engineers have always known that liftoff is a risky part of spaceflight.

375
00:38:10,460 --> 00:38:16,900
In the 1970s, they also learned about the hazards of fire in space.

376
00:38:18,420 --> 00:38:20,440
Okay, Houston, we've had a problem here.

377
00:38:21,900 --> 00:38:23,300
Houston, say again, please.

378
00:38:23,680 --> 00:38:24,380
Yes, sir.

379
00:38:24,381 --> 00:38:25,220
Houston, we've had a problem.

380
00:38:26,700 --> 00:38:33,840
April 1970, Apollo 13.

381
00:38:36,520 --> 00:38:42,420
Sparks touch off an explosive fire more than 200,000 miles from Earth.

382
00:38:47,140 --> 00:38:53,460
Miraculously, after three days in their crippled capsule, the astronauts return safely.

383
00:38:56,700 --> 00:39:05,180
The ordeal of Apollo 13 helped prompt research on exactly how fire behaves in zero gravity.

384
00:39:07,240 --> 00:39:12,800
The first experiments took place in 1974 on the Skylab space station.

385
00:39:14,440 --> 00:39:17,700
What astronauts discovered was astounding.

386
00:39:18,460 --> 00:39:25,560
There's simply no up or down in space, so you don't get the teardrop shape of a candle flame that we see here on Earth.

387
00:39:25,561 --> 00:39:30,660
instead in space it tends to be all round going in all directions equally.

388
00:39:31,760 --> 00:39:37,940
On Earth, warm air rises and cool air is drawn in to replace it from below.

389
00:39:40,460 --> 00:39:46,100
These currents caused by heat and gravity give flames their distinctive teardrop shape.

390
00:39:50,200 --> 00:39:53,240
In space, flames are round.

391
00:39:53,241 --> 00:39:59,260
without gravity hot air radiates in all directions instead of rising

392
00:39:59,261 --> 00:40:06,240
in the Skylab tests fires burned erratically

393
00:40:06,241 --> 00:40:15,820
without the phenomenon of hot air rising

394
00:40:15,821 --> 00:40:19,360
there was no natural current of cool air from below

395
00:40:19,361 --> 00:40:22,480
to feed fresh oxygen to the flame

396
00:40:23,240 --> 00:40:30,240
There was always a presumed margin of safety from those very early Skylab experiments.

397
00:40:30,241 --> 00:40:36,240
It was always presumed if we tested a material here on Earth and it burned a certain way,

398
00:40:36,241 --> 00:40:43,240
then if we burned that same material in space, that it would burn much more slowly or potentially not burn at all.

399
00:40:43,241 --> 00:40:47,240
This assumption turned out to be wrong,

400
00:40:47,241 --> 00:40:53,620
be wrong because the air inside Skylab's fire's test chamber was kept perfectly still.

401
00:40:54,780 --> 00:40:59,780
What we have since discovered is that if you have just a little bit of airflow, for example,

402
00:40:59,781 --> 00:41:04,640
from a ventilation system that's required for the astronauts on board, is that the fire can

403
00:41:04,641 --> 00:41:08,971
be much more robust, in fact, in space than here on Earth.

404
00:41:08,972 --> 00:41:11,660
Experiments aboard the space shuttle

405
00:41:11,661 --> 00:41:15,260
have discovered how droplets of fuel ignite in zero gravity.

406
00:41:17,240 --> 00:41:24,240
Heat and particles of soot rapidly spread the fire in every direction.

407
00:41:24,241 --> 00:41:31,240
In a pipe or tube, flames can race like a comet.

408
00:41:31,241 --> 00:41:40,240
In zero gravity, everyday objects burn in mysterious ways.

409
00:41:40,241 --> 00:41:44,240
This is a piece of ordinary paper.

410
00:41:44,241 --> 00:41:55,620
These experiments led NASA to take extreme measures to use specialized fireproof materials on the space shuttle.

411
00:41:57,480 --> 00:41:59,620
Even the smoke detectors are special.

412
00:42:00,480 --> 00:42:08,300
In our homes, we all put the smoke detectors near the ceiling because all the warm air and smoke rise and get into the vicinity of the smoke detector.

413
00:42:08,720 --> 00:42:12,980
This device, however, has to have a fan to pull the air into it.

414
00:42:14,240 --> 00:42:19,240
NASA's fire safety program has paid off.

415
00:42:19,241 --> 00:42:24,240
Several times equipment failures have generated sparks and smoke.

416
00:42:24,241 --> 00:42:31,240
However, astronauts have never had to use the fire extinguishers on board.

417
00:42:35,240 --> 00:42:39,240
The Russian space program has not been as fortunate.

418
00:42:39,241 --> 00:42:47,140
In February of 1997, aboard the space station Mir, an oxygen generator malfunctioned.

419
00:42:48,840 --> 00:42:57,160
The canister in which it was in ruptured and a blowtorch-like flame, fully one and a half feet long, was generated.

420
00:42:58,020 --> 00:43:04,560
The crew could not see their hand extended out in front of them, roughly one meter away from themselves,

421
00:43:04,800 --> 00:43:07,940
so it was a very dense cloud of smoke.

422
00:43:07,941 --> 00:43:14,060
They therefore then took extinguishers and simply tried to cool down or wet the surrounding areas

423
00:43:14,061 --> 00:43:18,940
to prevent the fire from actually melting a hole through the side of the wall of the mirror.

424
00:43:19,940 --> 00:43:24,940
The fire lasted 20 minutes, but astoundingly, no one was hurt.

425
00:43:24,941 --> 00:43:38,920
Experts are continuing to learn more about fire through experiments in space.

426
00:43:40,760 --> 00:43:46,360
This research is making spaceflight safer and is yielding important benefits on Earth,

427
00:43:46,480 --> 00:43:49,360
such as appliances that create less pollution.

428
00:43:49,361 --> 00:44:00,700
Experiments in microgravity, in weightlessness, are in fact the new frontier for researchers trying to understand fires.

429
00:44:01,700 --> 00:44:05,460
Fire is the foundation of modern civilization.

430
00:44:07,360 --> 00:44:11,680
85% of our energy comes from burning fossil fuels.

431
00:44:11,681 --> 00:44:19,540
As long as we depend on fire

432
00:44:19,541 --> 00:44:24,100
we'll also depend on the fearless individuals who step in

433
00:44:24,101 --> 00:44:28,100
when fire gets out of control

